{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming bc311b36, the checkpoint cache byte bound. Same reasoning as my claim on 118679d3: the repository-security lane your conditions told me to wait for merged as aa4f8756, and the configuration custody lane closed as d4f54392.\n\nI read the code at current main f4281e80 before claiming, so that I would not ask you to pay for work that had quietly landed. checkpointEventCache at internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:100 bounds itself by event count only: appendEvents compresses each full 4096-event chunk and appends the result to c.chunks, and the tail is bounded at the same count. Nothing measures bytes. A run of incompressible near-maximum payloads therefore accumulates without limit in memory, and the only byte limit in the file, maxCheckpointBytes at :32, is checked far later at marshal time, after the memory has already been taken. That is exactly the hole you described.\n\nI will bound accumulation in the cache and each in-progress chunk before that marshal limit, covering streamed full rebuilds and ordinary appends alike. On overflow the cache will fail closed, release its retained memory rather than hold it behind the error, and leave verified reads and the existing terminal oversized and rebuild-retry semantics untouched. I will not revive the legacy JSON retention or preflight work from ff999f86 or 49156978, and compact checkpoint v3, streamed rebuilds and eventWithoutPayload dedup all stay as they are.\n\nTests will be mutation-sensitive over single-chunk overflow, cumulative overflow, memory release and recovery after a verified full rebuild, and I will say which mutation each one kills. Work goes on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, with exact-head artifacts and an independent review carrying Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions."}